# 1Password secret references for the mobile auth key rings.
#
# Used directly, not copied:
#
#   op run --env-file docs/ops/mobile-auth-op-env.template -- \
#     pwsh -File ./scripts/ops/Check-MobileAuthKeyring.ps1 ... -UsePreinjectedRings
#
# `op run` resolves each reference and puts the value on the environment of the
# child process only. Nothing here is a secret: a reference is a pointer, which
# is why this file is committed and a resolved copy of it never is. Keeping the
# file in the repository rather than copying it to a local `.env` also removes
# the copy that could later be filled in by hand.
#
# ONLY the two rings belong here. The other six variables are ordinary
# parameters of the wrapper -- they are already visible in the Railway
# dashboard, and reading them off the command line is how an operator checks
# they typed them right. Adding them here would move that check out of sight.
#
# Which entry to point at is the operator's decision at the time:
#
#   ...Active     what is deployed now  -- section 3 step 4, the preflight check
#   ...Pending    the next candidate    -- section 3 steps 3 and 6
#
# Both lines below name Active. Point them at Pending for the steps that check
# the candidate. Contract and entry names: docs/ops/mobile-auth-key-rotation.md
# section 2.2.
#
# UNVERIFIED FROM THE REPOSITORY: the vault name contains spaces and the item
# name an em dash, and this environment cannot reach 1Password's documentation
# to confirm how those are handled in a reference. Resolving these two lines
# with `op read` is the first step of the real-vault check, and if either fails
# that is where it shows -- rename the item to something ASCII-only and update
# these lines and section 2.2 together. The vault name itself is approved and
# does not change.

MOBILE_AUTH_SIGNING_KEYS="op://Tomverse Production Secrets/Mobile Auth Keyrings — Active/signing-ring"
MOBILE_AUTH_REFRESH_PEPPERS="op://Tomverse Production Secrets/Mobile Auth Keyrings — Active/refresh-pepper-ring"
